@startuml Decorator-Visual-Objects
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
title The objects at run time (Design Patterns, p. 176) — and what each chain draws

object "aBorderDecorator" as bd1
object "aScrollDecorator" as sd1
object "aTextView" as tv1

bd1 --> sd1 : component
sd1 --> tv1 : component

note right of bd1
  new BorderDecorator(
    new ScrollDecorator(
      new TextView(...)))

  <b>+------+</b>
  <b>|hello^|</b>
  <b>|therev|</b>
  <b>+------+</b>

  scrollbar INSIDE the frame
end note

object "aScrollDecorator " as sd2
object "aBorderDecorator " as bd2
object "aTextView " as tv2

sd2 --> bd2 : component
bd2 --> tv2 : component

note right of sd2
  new ScrollDecorator(
    new BorderDecorator(
      new TextView(...)))

  <b>+-----+^</b>
  <b>|hello|#</b>
  <b>|there|#</b>
  <b>+-----+v</b>

  scrollbar OUTSIDE the frame
end note

note as N1
  Same three objects, same two classes, one difference:
  **which one holds which**. The chain is data, built by
  the caller, and it is where the behavior is decided.

  The client sees only a VisualComponent and cannot tell
  how deep the chain goes — that is the transparency the
  pattern buys, and the reason nesting has no limit.
end note

@enduml
